CHAPTER IV.
Spite
of remonstrance on my part, the imperative
countess, who had asserted her authority more than
once on our way to Laybach, insisted on the
company
of Miss Cunegunda Von Krakenpate, in an
evening walk around the town. Fearing that
Percie's
masculine stride would betray him, and
objecting
to lend myself to a farce with my valet, I opposed
the freak as long as it was courteousbut it was not
the first time I had learned that a spoiled woman
